############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML First, how many three-chip stacks with no repeated color have blue on the bottom and green in the middle?
[_]{3}{5}
And how many three-chip stacks with no repeated color have blue on the bottom and yellow in the middle?
[_]{3}{5}
In fact, for any stack with blue on the bottom and some other color in the middle, there are [_]{3}{5} possible stacks.
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML If we insist that blue is on the bottom, how many choices do we have for the color of the middle chip?
[_]{4}{5}
Combining this with the answer from the previous question, how many three-chip stacks with no repeated color have blue on the bottom?
[_]{12}{5}
END_PGML
Section::End();
############################################################ # Section ############################################################ Section::Begin("");
BEGIN_PGML Of course, we didn't need to start with blue on the bottom. How many choices do we have for the color of the bottom chip?
[_]{5}{5}
So how many three-chip stacks with no repeated color are there?
[_]{60}{5}
END_PGML
Section::End();
